message: If you use this work, please cite it as below.
title: 'After AFDC: No Detectable Cash-Assistance Channel from the China Shock to the Presidential Vote'
authors:
- name: PIATRA Institute
date-released: '2026-11-01'
url: https://piatra.institute/papers/after-afdc.pdf
repository-code: https://github.com/piatra-institute/papers-after-afdc
abstract: After the 1996 welfare law, families receiving cash assistance per 100 poor families with children fell in every contiguous state, from an average of 68 in 1995–96 to 20 in 2015–16, unevenly. If cash insurance shapes how voters respond to job loss, counties in states that lost more of it should have shifted further toward the Republican presidential candidate after the same local trade shock. The test regresses, on public data, the 2000–2016 change in the county Republican two-party share on instrumented commuting-zone exposure to Chinese import competition, interacted with the erosion of the state AFDC/TANF-to-poverty ratio, with state fixed effects. Across 3,106 counties the interaction is $-0.61$ points of Republican share per point of import penetration per standard deviation of erosion, with a 95% interval from $-1.56$ to 0.33. Across specifications the upper bound ranges from 0.20 to 1.03, against a shock effect of 1.30 at mean erosion, so only very large amplification is excluded. A first-stage check with county transfer data detects no response of family-assistance payments to the shock (0.98 dollars per capita per point, interval $-6.36$ to 8.32) and no moderation of that response by erosion ($-0.60$ per standard deviation, interval $-3.91$ to 2.72). In 2016 votes, the interval includes changes larger than the Michigan, Wisconsin and Pennsylvania margins in both directions, so county data cannot settle whether erosion's amplification of the trade-shock effect changed those states. The main limits are a moderator that mostly records 1996 coverage and a program that reached few displaced workers.
